you  [Music]  to pin down exactly what you're looking  for when you look for a role but I mean  it's just basic principles like I mean I  always enjoy being challenged by  something and feeling that I'm doing  something that I haven't done before so  that's probably the main thing that I  look for is something that's that's new  to me in really good script writing the  story is moved along and unfolds through  exchanges of character and through the  characters talking and sort of learning  about each other rather than sometimes  in scripts you know you're just reading  pages and pages of exposition and  characters explaining the plots and  actually a much more interesting at an  holy just better way of writing is is is  without exposition and where the story  is just being told through these  exchanges of character  I mean yeah just just bad writing just  um when you read a script that's like  kind of written with cliches and  sentimentality is something that I  always you know I can never stand in  films if I feel like the script is  trying to emotionally manipulate the  reader in a way that you know I'm not  naturally feeling that I always that's  something that sort of I find pretty  off-putting  preparation for every role is slightly  different but you know that's one of the  really fun parts of the job is is  working out how you need to prepare for  something I mean four to play Allen  Ginsberg obviously was a lot of work on  the accent and the voice and a lot of  reading about his life and and just  working with the director on things you  know for something like the musical I  did in New York on Broadway you know  that was learning to dance or and sing  and lessons like that so I mean it's I  like that my job always requires me to  prepare for something different free  time but ultimately you know the way you  break down a script is kind of the same  you know what what is your character  want in this scene what is he trying to  what effect is he trying to have on the  person that he's talking to when he says  that line and and what is his overall  kind of objective and if you have a very  clear idea I think of what those three  things are well that seemed that seems  to work for me anyways  every scene presents a different set of  challenges I mean there are always that  these scenes that you you worry about  the most the ones with you know huge  kind of emotional shifts and and things  like that and you know they can be very  hard but those the moments that you just  have to trust the director and trust the  other actors you're working with and and  give of yourself and be as emotionally  open and available as you can the  challenging scenes are the fun ones as  well I mean that's the thing is you know  that it's important to say that you know  the the more challenging scene is in a  way the more fun it is because the more  of my job I get to do  I think I learned from people around me  because I'm you know I'm now working  with people who haven't worked on nearly  as much as I have worked on but you know  they have worked in a completely  different way and have been through  drama school or have worked with you  know improvisation in a way that I  haven't normally so there's always you  know I'm kind of always looking around  to try and learn from everyone if I  could give one piece of advice it would  be that let's you know learn as much as  you can about how you the type of acting  you want to be the type of person you  want to be on set because you know you  can you can learn as much about how you  don't want to be as how you do want to  be from watching actors sometimes and I  was very lucky to have an amazing group  of people around me to watch and learn  from growing up so I think yeah just  just sort of be a sponge and take in as  much information as you can  gary oldman was somebody that I was  lucky enough to work with then I was  very young and he gave me great advice  and is still kind of real inspiration  